# Throughput floors for `scripts/protocol-matrix.sh --floors`.
#
# Each line pins one matrix row to either an absolute MiB/s number (`row=42`)
# or — preferred — a fraction of the same run's `tcp-plain` rate
# (`row=ratio:0.06`). Every row is measured back-to-back on one machine with
# the same 5 s / 1-stream window, so the ratio between two transports survives
# the host-to-host capacity differences that make an absolute number fragile
# on a shared CI runner.
#
# What this gate is for: a row that still bridges bytes but at a fraction of
# its previous rate. That is not hypothetical — KCP spent its whole life at
# 20.7 MiB/s on the ping-pong bench (65.5 MiB/s in the matrix after the FEC
# fix), and `mbps > 0` never noticed.
#
# Calibrated 2026-11-10 from three `compat` runs on the CI runner class
# (42844190428, 42844263709, 42844271249) plus one local macOS run. `tcp-plain`
# measured 636.1 / 1098.5 / 803.0 MiB/s in CI and 527.3 locally — a 2.1x
# capacity spread, which is why every other row is a ratio and not an absolute.
#
# Rule: each floor is half the LOWEST ratio observed on the platform that binds
# it, so one file serves both. CI (Linux) is the binding platform for tcp/kcp,
# macOS for the WebSocket rows — the 1-stream ping-pong in this matrix is a
# frame-latency measurement in which macOS loses about 2x on `ws` at the current
# buffer size (`docs/archive/notes/2026-11-10-bridge-buffer-64k-default.md` §4),
# and that shape is why the ws floors are lower than the Linux ratio would allow.
#
#   row           CI ratio (min)  macOS ratio  binding  floor
#   tcp-plain     636.1 MiB/s     527.3        -        absolute 300
#   tcp-tls       0.639           0.664        CI       0.32
#   tcp-tls-mux   0.548           0.587        CI       0.27
#   ws-plain      0.781           0.309        macOS    0.15
#   ws-tls        0.570           0.253        macOS    0.12
#   ws-tls-mux    0.503           0.334        macOS    0.16
#   wss           0.571           0.260        macOS    0.13
#   kcp-plain     0.100           0.121        CI       0.05
#   kcp-tls       0.131           0.126        macOS    0.06
#   kcp-tls-mux   0.049           0.099        CI       0.025
#   quic          0.298           0.167        macOS    0.08
#
# Before 2026-11-10 these numbers were measured through a benchmark peer with
# Nagle on, which cost a chunked shape ~8 ms per round trip and produced rows
# spread over 18-526 MiB/s (`docs/archive/notes/2026-11-10-nagle-in-the-benchmark.md`).
# Ratios from that harness are not comparable with these — re-derive, do not
# rescale.
#
# Re-tuning: the CI job uploads `protocol-matrix-rates.json`; take the new rates
# from it, apply the same rule (half the lowest observed ratio, on the binding
# platform), and record the run ids here. A floor that starts firing on a
# healthy run is a floor that is too high, not a machine that is too slow — the
# row's own `mbps > 0` check still guards connectivity, this file only guards
# rate.
tcp-plain=300
tcp-tls=ratio:0.32
tcp-tls-mux=ratio:0.27
ws-plain=ratio:0.15
ws-tls=ratio:0.12
ws-tls-mux=ratio:0.16
wss=ratio:0.13
kcp-plain=ratio:0.05
kcp-tls=ratio:0.06
kcp-tls-mux=ratio:0.025
quic=ratio:0.08
